# 76: the document of §50 written as ONE map -- the three statements
# merged, which is what `aontu fmt` would write for them -- answers
# differently in TypeScript: the template's key() gives "n" for f.x,
# not "x". Go answers "x" in both spellings.
#
# run: node ../../../ts/bin/aontu.js spread-key-through-deep-ref-merged.aon
# run: aontu-go spread-key-through-deep-ref-merged.aon
#
# expected (both ports, the answer both give the split spelling):
#   {"a":{"b":{"c":{"d":{"e":{"x":{"n":"x"}}}},"f":{"x":{"n":"x"}}}}}
#
# actual (Go): as expected
# actual (TypeScript):
#   {"a":{"b":{"c":{"d":{"e":{"x":{"n":"x"}}}},"f":{"x":{"n":"n"}}}}}
#
# Found by the formatter's lawful-tier check (docs/design/FMT.0.md
# §7.3): merging §50's three statements into one map is a rewrite the
# check refused in TypeScript and accepted in Go, so `aontu fmt`
# writes that repro differently in the two ports -- the split
# spelling kept in TypeScript, the merged one in Go.
a: b: { c:d:e:$.a.b.f f:{ &: { n:key() } x:{} } }
